<p class="eyebrow">documentation / introduction</p>
<h1>How Ashplug works</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">Ashplug is an AI-powered shell assistant. It turns natural-language intent into a typed execution plan, then keeps validation, safety, execution, and history inside the Rust application.</p>
<div class="doc-callout"><strong>Core boundary</strong><span>The AI proposes data. It never executes a command, changes policy, or approves its own operation.</span></div>
<h2>Request lifecycle</h2>
<ol class="lifecycle">
  <li><b>01</b><span>CLI receives a natural-language task.</span></li>
  <li><b>02</b><span>A provider returns a structured plan.</span></li>
  <li><b>03</b><span>The planner validates the plan.</span></li>
  <li><b>04</b><span>The safety engine returns allow, approval, or deny.</span></li>
  <li><b>05</b><span>Approved commands execute without a shell.</span></li>
  <li><b>06</b><span>Output and decisions are recorded in history.</span></li>
</ol>
<h2>What it supports</h2>
<p>The current MVP supports OpenAI-compatible, Ollama, and mock providers; Bash and Zsh integration; configurable safety policy; command timeouts and bounded output; SQLite history; and bounded recovery after failed steps.</p>
